# Multi-Tenant Implementation Plan — DSP True Connector

> **Status**: Draft for team review  
> **Version**: 0.6.9-SNAPSHOT  
> **Document prepared by**: GitHub Copilot analysis session  
> **Revision 1**: DAPS references removed — DAPS is being removed from codebase; connector authentication replacement TBD (see Q8 in §15)  
> **Revision 2**: Default "Engineering" tenant bootstrapped on every startup; the connector is not operational without at least one enabled tenant

---

## 1. Executive Summary

The DSP True Connector is currently a **single-tenant** Spring Boot application. Each deployment represents exactly one connector identity and serves exactly one set of catalogs, negotiations and data transfers. The goal of this initiative is to evolve it into a **multi-tenant** application where:

- A single deployed instance hosts multiple independent tenants.
- Each tenant has its own catalogs, datasets, negotiations, agreements, and data transfers.
- Tenants cannot see or use each other's resources.
- A super-admin role can see and manage all tenants' resources.
- Each tenant carries its own connector identity (connector ID).
- **A default tenant named "Engineering" is always bootstrapped on startup** — the connector is not operational without at least one enabled tenant.
- Two tenant-identification strategies are provided for DSP protocol endpoints (see §5).

---

## 2. Current Architecture Overview

### 2.1 Module Structure

| Module | Purpose |
|--------|---------|
| `connector` | Main Spring Boot app — wires all modules, security, user management |
| `catalog` | Catalog, Dataset, Distribution, DataService, Offer management |
| `negotiation` | Contract negotiation lifecycle and policy enforcement |
| `data-transfer` | Transfer process management (HTTP Push/Pull, S3, FTP) |
| `tools` | Shared: Artifact, ApplicationProperty, S3, Audit, SSL |

### 2.2 MongoDB Collections (all currently single-tenant)

| Collection | Model |
|------------|-------|
| `users` | User (connector, admin, user roles) |
| `catalogs` | Catalog |
| `datasets` | Dataset |
| `distributions` | Distribution |
| `dataservices` | DataService |
| `artifacts` | Artifact |
| `contract_negotiations` | ContractNegotiation |
| `agreements` | Agreement |
| `policy_enforcements` | PolicyEnforcement |
| `transfer_process` | TransferProcess |
| `transfer_request_messages` | TransferRequestMessage |
| `transfer_start_messages` | TransferStartMessage |
| `transfer_states` | TransferArtifactState |
| `application_properties` | ApplicationProperty |
| `audit_events` | AuditEvent |
| `bucket_credentials` | BucketCredentialsEntity |

### 2.3 Authentication

- **API endpoints** (`/api/**`): Basic Auth (username + password stored in `users` collection) or JWT.
- **Protocol endpoints** (`/catalog/**`, `/negotiations/**`, `/transfers/**`): connector-to-connector authentication (DAPS is being removed; replacement TBD).
- **Roles**: `ROLE_ADMIN` (API management), `ROLE_CONNECTOR` (DSP protocol), `ROLE_USER` (no special privileges).
- Spring Security is configured in `WebSecurityConfig.java` in the connector module.

### 2.4 Key Design Gaps for Multi-Tenancy

1. No `tenantId` field on any domain object.
2. No `Tenant` model or repository.
3. No tenant context propagation between security layer and service/repository layers.
4. Single connector identity (`application.connectorid`) per deployment.
5. Single S3 bucket per deployment.
6. `CatalogService.getCatalog()` returns `allCatalogs.get(0)` — hard-coded single catalog assumption.

---

## 3. Design Decisions

| # | Decision | Choice | Rationale |
|---|----------|--------|-----------|
| D1 | **Tenant identification on DSP protocol endpoints** | Two solutions: HTTP header OR request body filter (see §5) | Protocol URLs are standard DSP — cannot be changed |
| D2 | **User–tenant relationship** | One user belongs to exactly one tenant | Simplest model; avoids session complexity |
| D3 | **Data isolation strategy** | Shared MongoDB collections, `tenantId` field on every document | Minimal schema change; leverages existing Spring Data infrastructure |
| D4 | **Admin role** | New `ROLE_SUPER_ADMIN` — not tied to any tenant | Separates operational from tenant-level admin |
| D5 | **Tenant identity** | Each tenant has its own `connectorId` | Stronger identity isolation; required for protocol compliance |
| D6 | **Tenant management** | Default "Engineering" tenant guaranteed on startup; additional tenants via `initial_data.json` bootstrap + runtime CRUD API (`/api/v1/tenants`) | The connector must have at least one operational tenant; consistency with existing property management pattern |
| D7 | **S3 artifact storage** | Two solutions presented (see §8) | Let team decide based on operational preferences |

---

## 4. New Domain Model: Tenant

### 4.1 `Tenant` Model

```java
@Document(collection = "tenants")
public class Tenant implements Serializable {

    @Id
    private String id;                     // tenant identifier (e.g. "tenant-a")

    private String name;                   // human-readable name
    private String description;

    // DSP Identity
    private String connectorId;            // unique per tenant (was application.connectorid)
    private String callbackAddress;        // base URL for this tenant's outgoing calls

    // S3 Storage (see §8 for two options)
    private String s3BucketName;           // Option A: separate bucket per tenant
    private String s3ObjectKeyPrefix;      // Option B: shared bucket, "tenantId/" prefix

    // Status
    private boolean enabled;

    @CreatedDate private Instant created;
    @LastModifiedDate private Instant modified;
    @CreatedBy private String createdBy;
    @LastModifiedBy private String lastModifiedBy;
    @Version private Long version;
}
```

### 4.2 `TenantRepository`

```java
@Repository
public interface TenantRepository extends MongoRepository<Tenant, String> {
    Optional<Tenant> findByConnectorId(String connectorId);
    List<Tenant> findByEnabled(boolean enabled);
}
```

### 4.3 Updated `User` Model

Add `tenantId` field. Super-admins leave it `null`:

```java
@Document(collection = "users")
public class User implements UserDetails {
    // ... existing fields ...
    private String tenantId;   // null → ROLE_SUPER_ADMIN; non-null → belongs to that tenant
}
```

---

## 5. Tenant Resolution on DSP Protocol Endpoints

Protocol endpoint URLs (`/catalog/request`, `/negotiations/**`, `/transfers/**`) are defined by the Dataspace Protocol specification and **cannot be changed**. Two solutions are provided; the team may choose either or support both:

### Solution A — HTTP Header `X-Tenant-ID`

The consumer connector sends a custom header:

```
POST /catalog/request HTTP/1.1
Authorization: Bearer <token>
X-Tenant-ID: tenant-a
Content-Type: application/json

{ "@context": "...", "@type": "dspace:CatalogRequestMessage" }
```

**Pros**: Simple to implement; transparent to the DSP message body.  
**Cons**: Non-standard — requires the calling connector to know the tenant ID.

### Solution B — `CatalogRequestMessage.filter` Field

The `CatalogRequestMessage` already contains an optional `filter` (or `filter` extension) field. Encode the tenant ID there:

```json
{
  "@context": "https://w3id.org/dspace/2024/1/context.json",
  "@type": "dspace:CatalogRequestMessage",
  "dspace:filter": { "tenantId": "tenant-a" }
}
```

For non-catalog protocol endpoints (negotiations, transfers) — which do not carry a message body tenant filter — the `X-Tenant-ID` header **must** still be used.

**Pros**: DSP-body-aligned; no extra header.  
**Cons**: Only applicable to catalog; header still needed elsewhere.

### `TenantContextFilter` (New)

A new servlet filter extracts the tenant ID from either source and stores it in a `TenantContext` (thread-local):

```java
@Component
public class TenantContextFilter extends OncePerRequestFilter {

    @Override
    protected void doFilterInternal(HttpServletRequest request, HttpServletResponse response,
                                    FilterChain filterChain) throws ServletException, IOException {
        // 1. Try header first
        String tenantId = request.getHeader("X-Tenant-ID");
        
        // 2. For authenticated API calls, derive from Security context (user's tenantId)
        //    — this covers all /api/** endpoints automatically.
        
        TenantContextHolder.setTenantId(tenantId);
        try {
            filterChain.doFilter(request, response);
        } finally {
            TenantContextHolder.clear();
        }
    }
}
```

### `TenantContextHolder` (New, in `tools` module)

```java
public class TenantContextHolder {
    private static final ThreadLocal<String> TENANT_ID = new ThreadLocal<>();
    
    public static void setTenantId(String tenantId) { TENANT_ID.set(tenantId); }
    public static String getTenantId() { return TENANT_ID.get(); }
    public static void clear() { TENANT_ID.remove(); }
}
```

---

## 6. Role & Authorization Changes

### 6.1 Updated Role Enum

```java
public enum Role {
    ROLE_USER,          // regular user (no special privileges)
    ROLE_ADMIN,         // tenant-scoped admin (manages own tenant's resources via /api/**)
    ROLE_CONNECTOR,     // DSP protocol connector-to-connector calls
    ROLE_SUPER_ADMIN    // NEW: cross-tenant admin, not bound to any tenant
}
```

### 6.2 Updated `WebSecurityConfig`

```java
.authorizeHttpRequests((authorize) -> {
    authorize
        .requestMatchers("/env", "/actuator/**")
            .hasAnyRole("ADMIN", "SUPER_ADMIN")
        .requestMatchers("/connector/**", "/negotiations/**", "/catalog/**", "/transfers/**")
            .hasRole("CONNECTOR")
        .requestMatchers("/api/v1/tenants/**")
            .hasRole("SUPER_ADMIN")           // tenant CRUD — super-admin only
        .requestMatchers("/api/**")
            .hasAnyRole("ADMIN", "SUPER_ADMIN")
        .anyRequest().permitAll();
})
```

### 6.3 Security Context to Tenant Context Propagation

After successful authentication, the `TenantContextFilter` (or a `SecurityContextAwareFilter`) populates `TenantContextHolder` from the authenticated `User.tenantId`. For super-admins, an optional `X-Tenant-ID` header can override (allowing super-admins to act on any tenant's behalf).

---

## 7. Data Isolation — Adding `tenantId` to All Collections

### 7.1 Collections that must gain a `tenantId` field

Every collection that stores tenant-specific data:

| Collection | Change |
|------------|--------|
| `catalogs` | Add `String tenantId` |
| `datasets` | Add `String tenantId` |
| `distributions` | Add `String tenantId` |
| `dataservices` | Add `String tenantId` |
| `artifacts` | Add `String tenantId` |
| `contract_negotiations` | Add `String tenantId` |
| `agreements` | Add `String tenantId` |
| `policy_enforcements` | Add `String tenantId` |
| `transfer_process` | Add `String tenantId` |
| `transfer_request_messages` | Add `String tenantId` |
| `transfer_start_messages` | Add `String tenantId` |
| `transfer_states` | Add `String tenantId` |
| `application_properties` | Add `String tenantId` (tenant-specific config) |
| `audit_events` | Add `String tenantId` |
| `bucket_credentials` | Add `String tenantId` |

Collections that remain **global** (no tenantId needed):

| Collection | Reason |
|------------|--------|
| `users` | Already has `tenantId`; super-admin has `null` |
| `tenants` | New global collection |

### 7.2 Repository-Level Tenant Filtering

All repository query methods must include `tenantId` in the filter. There are two viable approaches:

#### Option A — Extend Custom Repository Interface

Add a tenant-aware find method to each repository or to `GenericDynamicFilterRepository`:

```java
public interface TenantAwareRepository<T, ID> {
    List<T> findAllByTenantId(String tenantId);
    Optional<T> findByIdAndTenantId(ID id, String tenantId);
    // etc.
}
```

#### Option B — Spring Data MongoDB `MongoTemplate` with automatic query enrichment (Recommended)

Create a Spring `MongoCallback` or extend repositories using a `TenantAwareRepositoryImpl` that automatically appends `{ tenantId: "<current>" }` to every query using `MongoTemplate`. This is analogous to Hibernate's `@Filter` / multi-tenancy support:

```java
@Aspect
@Component
public class TenantQueryEnrichmentAspect {
    // Around advice on MongoRepository methods:
    // Inject TenantContextHolder.getTenantId() into query Criteria
}
```

**Recommendation**: Use Spring Data's `MongoTemplate` with query criteria enrichment via a custom `TenantAwareMongoRepositoryFactoryBean` — this keeps repository interfaces unchanged.

### 7.3 Service-Layer Tenant Enforcement

All service methods that currently ignore tenant must be updated. Example:

```java
// BEFORE
public Catalog getCatalog() {
    List<Catalog> allCatalogs = repository.findAll();
    return allCatalogs.get(0);
}

// AFTER
public Catalog getCatalog() {
    String tenantId = TenantContextHolder.getTenantId();
    List<Catalog> catalogs = repository.findAllByTenantId(tenantId);
    if (catalogs.isEmpty()) throw new CatalogErrorException("Catalog not available");
    return catalogs.get(0);
}
```

### 7.4 Super-Admin Bypass

Service methods check if the caller is a super-admin and, if so, skip the `tenantId` filter (or apply an optional `X-Tenant-ID` override):

```java
private String resolveEffectiveTenantId() {
    Authentication auth = SecurityContextHolder.getContext().getAuthentication();
    if (auth.getAuthorities().contains(new SimpleGrantedAuthority("ROLE_SUPER_ADMIN"))) {
        // Super-admin: use header-provided tenantId if given, else null = all tenants
        return TenantContextHolder.getTenantId(); // may be null → no filter
    }
    // Regular tenant user: always filter by their own tenantId
    User user = (User) auth.getPrincipal();
    return user.getTenantId();
}
```

---

## 8. S3 Artifact Storage — Two Options

### Option A — Separate Bucket per Tenant

- Each tenant has a dedicated S3 bucket (`s3.bucketName` stored on the `Tenant` entity).
- `BucketCredentialsService` provisions separate IAM credentials per bucket.
- Strong physical isolation; easiest permission boundary.
- **Operational cost**: More buckets to manage.

```java
// Tenant A: bucket "dsp-true-connector-tenant-a"
// Tenant B: bucket "dsp-true-connector-tenant-b"
```

**`InitialDataLoader`** must provision a bucket for each tenant at startup.

### Option B — Shared Bucket with Tenant-Prefixed Object Keys

- Single bucket; all object keys are prefixed with `tenantId/`:
  - `tenant-a/urn:uuid:fdc45798-...`
  - `tenant-b/urn:uuid:fdc45798-...`
- `S3ClientService` is updated to prepend the tenant prefix from `TenantContextHolder`.
- Lower operational overhead.
- **Risk**: IAM policy must be carefully scoped if S3 presigned URLs are used (URLs could expose cross-tenant paths).

```java
// In S3ClientService (Option B):
private String buildObjectKey(String baseKey) {
    String tenantId = TenantContextHolder.getTenantId();
    return tenantId != null ? tenantId + "/" + baseKey : baseKey;
}
```

**Recommendation**: Present both options to the team. Option A is safer for production; Option B is simpler for development/staging.

---

## 9. Per-Tenant Connector Identity

Each tenant must have its own unique `connectorId`. This replaces the current global `application.connectorid` property.

### 9.1 `Tenant` Entity — Connector ID

```java
private String connectorId;      // e.g. "urn:connector:tenant-a"
private String callbackAddress;  // base callback URL for this tenant's outgoing protocol calls
```

### 9.2 Connector ID Resolution

All services that currently read `application.connectorid` (e.g., negotiation and transfer services that set `assigner`/`assignee`) must be updated to resolve the connector ID from the active tenant via `TenantContextHolder`:

```java
// BEFORE
@Value("${application.connectorid}")
private String connectorId;

// AFTER
private String resolveConnectorId() {
    String tenantId = TenantContextHolder.getTenantId();
    return tenantService.findById(tenantId).getConnectorId();
}
```

### 9.3 SSL / Outgoing Requests

Per-tenant SSL certificates for outgoing connector-to-connector HTTP calls (currently configured via Spring SSL bundles) should be revisited once the connector authentication strategy is finalised (DAPS is being removed — the replacement mechanism will drive this requirement).

> **Note**: Section 9.3 is intentionally left open pending the decision on what replaces DAPS for connector authentication.

---

## 10. Tenant Management API

### 10.1 New Endpoints (`/api/v1/tenants`) — Super-Admin only

| Method | Path | Description |
|--------|------|-------------|
| `GET` | `/api/v1/tenants` | List all tenants |
| `GET` | `/api/v1/tenants/{id}` | Get tenant by ID |
| `POST` | `/api/v1/tenants` | Create new tenant |
| `PUT` | `/api/v1/tenants/{id}` | Update tenant |
| `DELETE` | `/api/v1/tenants/{id}` | Disable/remove tenant |
| `POST` | `/api/v1/tenants/{id}/enable` | Enable tenant |
| `POST` | `/api/v1/tenants/{id}/disable` | Disable tenant |

### 10.2 Bootstrap via `initial_data.json`

Extend the existing `initial_data.json` bootstrap format with a new `tenants` collection section, consistent with how users and catalogs are currently seeded.

**The "Engineering" tenant is the mandatory default tenant.** It is always created by `InitialDataLoader` on startup, independently of the profile-specific `initial_data.json` file. Additional tenants may be seeded through `initial_data.json` or created at runtime via the API.

Example `tenants` entry in `initial_data.json`:

```json
{
  "tenants": [
    {
      "_id": "engineering",
      "name": "Engineering",
      "description": "Default Engineering tenant — created on every startup",
      "connectorId": "urn:connector:engineering",
      "callbackAddress": "http://localhost:8090",
      "enabled": true
    }
  ]
}
```

### 10.3 Updated `InitialDataLoader`

On startup the loader performs the following steps **in order**:

1. **Ensure the "Engineering" tenant exists** — create it with its default values if the document with `_id = "engineering"` is not already present. This step runs unconditionally before any profile-specific data is processed.
2. Load profile-specific `initial_data-<profile>.json` and for each collection entry:
   a. Create the document if it does not already exist (existing behaviour).
   b. For tenant-scoped collections (`catalogs`, `datasets`, `artifacts`, etc.), set `tenantId` from the JSON document; fall back to `"engineering"` if the field is absent.
3. Provision S3 bucket/prefix for each enabled tenant (based on chosen Option A or B).
4. Validate that at least one enabled tenant exists — fail fast with a clear error message if none is found.

```java
// Pseudocode — InitialDataLoader
private void ensureEngineeringTenant() {
    if (tenantRepository.findById("engineering").isEmpty()) {
        Tenant engineering = Tenant.Builder.newInstance()
            .id("engineering")
            .name("Engineering")
            .description("Default Engineering tenant")
            .connectorId("urn:connector:engineering")
            .callbackAddress(environment.getProperty("application.callback.address"))
            .enabled(true)
            .build();
        tenantRepository.save(engineering);
        log.info("Default 'Engineering' tenant created.");
    }
}
```

---

## 11. Audit Events

The `AuditEvent` model must gain a `tenantId` field so audit logs are scoped correctly:

```java
@Document(collection = "audit_events")
public class AuditEvent {
    // ... existing fields ...
    private String tenantId;   // populated from TenantContextHolder
}
```

`AuditEventPublisher` reads the current `TenantContextHolder.getTenantId()` when building events.

The `/api/v1/audit` endpoint filters results by tenant for regular admins; super-admin sees all.

---

## 12. Protocol Endpoint Changes

### 12.1 `CatalogController` (protocol — `/catalog/request`)

```java
@PostMapping(path = "/request")
public ResponseEntity<JsonNode> getCatalog(
        @RequestHeader(required = false) String authorization,
        @RequestHeader(value = "X-Tenant-ID", required = false) String tenantIdHeader,
        @RequestBody JsonNode jsonBody) {

    // Resolve tenant: header first, then body filter
    String tenantId = resolveTenantId(tenantIdHeader, jsonBody);
    TenantContextHolder.setTenantId(tenantId);

    CatalogSerializer.deserializeProtocol(jsonBody, CatalogRequestMessage.class);
    Catalog catalog = catalogService.getCatalog(); // now reads from TenantContextHolder
    // ...
}
```

### 12.2 Negotiation & Transfer Protocol Controllers

These controllers do not carry a DSP message body with a filter, so they rely exclusively on the `X-Tenant-ID` header (extracted by `TenantContextFilter` before the filter chain reaches the controller).

---

## 13. Migration Strategy for Existing Data

Because `tenantId` is new and all existing documents lack it, a migration is required:

### Option A — "Engineering" Tenant (Recommended)
- The "Engineering" tenant (id: `"engineering"`) is always created on startup (see §10.3).
- A MongoDB migration script (Mongock or manual) updates all existing documents that lack a `tenantId` to set `tenantId = "engineering"`.
- Existing users without a `tenantId` are assigned to the `"engineering"` tenant.
- This is the natural choice: the existing single-tenant data logically belongs to the Engineering tenant.

### Option B — Allow null tenantId as "legacy"
- Queries treat `null tenantId` as "unassigned legacy data" visible only to super-admins until migrated.
- Lower risk but adds complexity to query logic.

**Recommendation**: Option A — the "Engineering" tenant is the canonical home for all pre-existing data.

---

## 14. Implementation Phases

### Phase 1 — Foundation (tools + connector module)
1. Create `Tenant` model, `TenantRepository` (tools module).
2. Create `TenantContextHolder` (tools module).
3. Add `ROLE_SUPER_ADMIN` to `Role` enum.
4. Add `tenantId` field to `User` model.
5. Create `TenantContextFilter` (connector module).
6. Update `WebSecurityConfig` with new authorization rules.
7. Create `TenantAPIController` (`/api/v1/tenants`) — super-admin only.
8. Update `InitialDataLoader`: add `ensureEngineeringTenant()` called first on startup; seed all subsequent documents with `tenantId = "engineering"` as the fallback; add startup validation that at least one enabled tenant exists.
9. Write unit + integration tests for tenant management.

### Phase 2 — Data isolation (catalog module)
1. Add `tenantId` field to `Catalog`, `Dataset`, `Distribution`, `DataService`, `Artifact`.
2. Add `tenantId` filter to all `CatalogRepository`, `DatasetRepository`, etc. queries.
3. Update `CatalogService`, `DatasetService`, etc. to use `TenantContextHolder`.
4. Update `CatalogController` (protocol) to extract tenant from header / body filter.
5. Update `CatalogAPIController` (management) to enforce tenant scope.
6. Write unit + integration tests.

### Phase 3 — Data isolation (negotiation module)
1. Add `tenantId` to `ContractNegotiation`, `Agreement`, `PolicyEnforcement`.
2. Update repositories and services.
3. Update negotiation protocol controllers.
4. Update negotiation API controllers.
5. Write unit + integration tests.

### Phase 4 — Data isolation (data-transfer module)
1. Add `tenantId` to `TransferProcess`, `TransferRequestMessage`, `TransferStartMessage`, `TransferArtifactState`.
2. Update repositories and services.
3. Update data transfer protocol controllers.
4. Update data transfer API controllers.
5. Write unit + integration tests.

### Phase 5 — Per-Tenant Connector Identity
1. Move `connectorId` from `application.properties` to `Tenant` entity.
2. Update all services reading `application.connectorid` to resolve from the active tenant via `TenantContextHolder`.
3. Update `callbackAddress` resolution to be per-tenant.
4. Write tests.

### Phase 6 — Per-tenant S3 Storage
1. Decide on Option A (separate bucket) or Option B (prefixed key) — or implement both as config.
2. Update `S3ClientService` to be tenant-aware.
3. Update `InitialDataLoader` to provision S3 per tenant.
4. Update `BucketCredentialsService`.
5. Write tests.

### Phase 7 — Migration & Audit
1. Write MongoDB migration script (Mongock recommended) to set `tenantId = "engineering"` on all existing documents that lack the field.
2. Update `AuditEvent` with `tenantId`.
3. Update `AuditEventPublisher` to populate from `TenantContextHolder`.
4. Update audit API endpoint to filter by tenant.
5. Run integration tests end-to-end with multiple tenants.

### Phase 8 — Documentation & Testing
1. Update `README.md` with multi-tenant setup instructions.
2. Add Postman collection examples for multi-tenant scenarios.
3. Update `initial_data.json` examples.
4. End-to-end integration test with 2 tenants + super-admin.
5. DSP TCK validation per tenant.

---

## 15. Open Questions / Decisions Needed by Team

| # | Question | Options |
|---|----------|---------|
| Q1 | Which tenant-ID resolution strategy for protocol endpoints? | Header (`X-Tenant-ID`) and/or body filter — both provided in design, pick one or support both |
| Q2 | S3 isolation: separate bucket (Option A) or prefixed keys (Option B)? | Both described in §8 — team to decide |
| Q3 | Data migration: Mongock library or manual script? | Mongock integrates cleanly with Spring Boot |
| Q4 | Should `application_properties` be per-tenant? E.g., each tenant could have different `application.protocol.authentication.enabled` | Yes/No decision impacts Phase 1 scope |
| Q5 | Should the Proxy API (`/api/v1/proxy`) forward requests with `X-Tenant-ID`? | Likely yes — ProxyAPIService needs to be tenant-aware |
| Q6 | Automatic negotiation (`application.automatic.negotiation`) — per-tenant flag or global? | Likely per-tenant — impacts Phase 3 |
| Q7 | FTP server — per-tenant or shared? | Likely per-tenant (separate ports or tenant-aware auth) |
| Q8 | Connector authentication replacing DAPS — what mechanism? | Decision pending; will impact §9.3 (per-tenant SSL) |

---

## 16. Risks & Mitigations

| Risk | Impact | Mitigation |
|------|--------|-----------|
| `DBRef` cascade — `Catalog` references `Dataset` by `@DBRef`; tenant filtering on catalog alone may load cross-tenant datasets | High | Ensure `tenantId` is on every referenced collection; validate at service layer |
| Forgetting to filter in a new query method | Medium | Implement repository-level aspect or factory bean that auto-injects tenant filter |
| Performance — adding `tenantId` criteria to every query | Low | Create MongoDB compound indexes: `(tenantId, _id)`, `(tenantId, state)`, etc. on frequently queried collections |
| Concurrent tenant context leakage in async operations | Medium | `TenantContextHolder` is `ThreadLocal`; Spring `@Async` tasks must propagate context via `TaskDecorator` |
| Protocol TCK compliance — `X-Tenant-ID` header is non-standard | Low | TCK tests run against a single tenant; keep header optional with sensible default |

---

## 17. Recommended MongoDB Indexes (New)

After adding `tenantId`, the following compound indexes are recommended:

```javascript
